[Music]  hello  and welcome to the how the light gets in  festival 2021  i'm your host for this event my name is  rana mitter and today i'll be exploring  the ark of life with a very special  guest  ayan hersey ali now ayan is a very very  well-known writer and thinker she's a  somali-born  classical liberal she's an advocate for  free speech  she's an activist for the right of women  and of girls  she's a public intellectual with a very  powerful voice  she's a former muslim who has rejected  her form of faith and become  an atheist and she's since been a vocal  critic of both islam  and the identitarian left she's written  several best-selling books including  infidel  nomad heretic and the challenge of noah  and her new book prey which i have right  here  just came out this year i unwelcome to  how the light gets in it's a pleasure to  be able to chat to you tonight  i think the first question i have to ask  you because i have to ask that everyone  during this  strange year is where have you been  during the pandemic and  how's it been for you well rana thank  you very very much for having me  on uh so we have been my husband and my  children i we  have been sheltering in uh the nor  well it's in the west of america  as you know with my safety issues i am  not very keen on  seeing exactly where we were uh but uh  quite uh isolated  and it's the type of uh place where you  would say  even before the pandemic  there was a great deal of distancing but  it was perfect for us  have you found that being i mean as  you've said because of  safety issues and we're going to talk  i'm sure more about that during our  conversation in terms of  your personal safety you perhaps got  used to having to be very wary about  people around you  before the rest of us did for different  reasons but have you found that  essentially being enforced into a kind  of isolation  has been helpful to your work style has  it been problematic has it not changed  things that much for you  i think it did change things and did  change things quite dramatically uh i  so this started happening in 2002 for me  that's a long time ago and uh you know  i was in my early 30s uh was having a  great deal of fun  going out and socializing i used to do a  lot of  outdoors biking and stuff like that and  a lot of it had to stop  so i had to adjust and at the time you  know there were a few individuals who  were going through that  but now with the pandemic and again  with radical islamist threats on the  rise  more and more people are familiar with  that pattern uh it's been a change  but i think the the main thing is for  one to learn to adapt to it  psychologically so it doesn't get you  down  and adapting psychologically is  something that in different ways and  you know for most of us very different  reasons from you have had to do one way  or another so  perhaps there will be kind of some wider  sense of empathy about how much  things have to change you already  mentioned or at least referred to  various things that have happened  in your prominent at times controversial  at times  uh very very kind of action-packed  experience as i think as a politician as  a public intellectual but for those of  us watching for those people  watching tonight here at the festival  who perhaps you know know your name but  don't know that much about your  background  perhaps we could take a few minutes just  to think a little bit about you know  this very  long trajectory that you've made from  birth in somalia to where you are  now in the uh in the united states  and just you know as a quick recap  people who know your stories know a  little bit about this but  your background your upbringing was in  somalia that that was where you started  out  it's where i started out and i was very  young i was about eight or so years old  when my family left  somalia for saudi arabia and we lived  there for a year and then we went to  ethiopia for a year and a half  and then for about 10 or so years in  nairobi kenya  and by then it's what  1992 and my father who had been gone for  10 years  came back and you know long story short  he  arranged uh for me to marry someone of  his choice i didn't agree with that  and i found an opportunity to escape  when i was uh  supposed to go through germany so this  man that he married me off to  was a citizen of canada and instead of  going to canada  from germany i took a train from bonn  to amsterdam and this was on this was  july  24 1992 and and i never looked back  so that's really the kind of turning  point in your in your life and  you spent um some time in the  netherlands  uh becoming very involved in what you  might call  broadly speaking politics but also  particular politics to do  with different communities with the  interactions between them and sometimes  the confrontations between them and  you're a member of parliament for a  while as  well that's some years ago now but  looking back on that you know how do you  think about that period  so i look uh you know i look back i was  in holland for 14 years  and i look back on those 14 years and i  just think they were  fantastic years for me it was you know i  came to a country that prided itself on  freedom and gave me lots of  opportunities  and i took those opportunities i learned  the language i became a translator  interpreter  i went to the university of leyden and  at some point  i went to work for a think tank and at  the think tank they were struggling with  the question of  integration we call it a simulation here  in the u.s  but integrating into society and  a number of my colleagues my senior  colleagues said well how did you do it  you've been here only for 10 years at  that point  um and i threw myself in trying to  answer that question grapple with that  question  and it was a line that led me to think  after i had looked into  some of the north african you know  immigrants from north africa and their  children  from turkey and their children and then  other places iraq somalia syria  afghanistan those places  and i just pointed out you know if you  allow if you emancipate these women  these girls and women you allow them to  finish school  and they can choose their mates and they  if they  get given the same opportunities as  young white dutch girls  then the integration problem in a  generational or so will completely  resolve itself  at the time i didn't realize how  controversial that was  and i still think it's it's a truism but  it's still controversial so  let me dig a little bit into you know  how you came into and then articulated  those views because you've already  mentioned from your earlier life that  you took this  very brave and i should imagine pretty  unusual decision you know  when this potential forced marriage was  being put before you that you know as  you say you pretty much got on train  and and didn't come back so you already  clearly had something going on inside  you that  sent you in that direction even before  you got to the netherlands  and then when you were in the  netherlands clearly what you saw there  hugely influenced your thinking so you  know how much of this was here  there from the start and how much was  developed by your experience  once you've got to to to holland so  i want to say first of all it wasn't  just oh i got on a train and  left and never looked back it was much  more complicated than that  obviously growing up as a young girl i  had been exposed to  um you know my mother and the way her  life seemed to be falling apart she  never had any control over anything  she didn't she couldn't read or write  and that made her  dependent on my father and my father  abandoned us he was gone  for ten years after he had brought my  mother to a country  that to her was strange she didn't speak  the language she couldn't uh  she couldn't rely on her relatives the  way she was lying on her relatives in  somalia that being saudi arabia  well in saudi arabia also she felt she  was quite a stranger there but at least  we had some relatives there  it's a muslim country etc my mother  thought she was happy in saudi arabia  the rest of us were not  but i mean there was this whole process  even my  classmates at school some of them were  forced into marriages or went into  arranged marriages at about  age 15 16 17 i saw these young girls  you know become little old women before  my very eyes  they gained weight they had children  they had become i felt that their lives  were stunted  and so it was clear to me what i didn't  want  i didn't want to be like my mother i  didn't want to be like those classmates  and then so when i was married off to  this man from canada  i thought if there's a way i can get out  of this  i will and once i saw that opportunity  in germany i thought well this is my  chance either do it now  or it's never going to happen it's an  extraordinary story i suppose what i  meant by that was that as you said you  had  classmates uh you had other people  around you who were faced with similar  choices but they didn't  take the same you know very brave step  that you did so  there must be something quite personal  so about you that you felt motivated  this way in a way that  those around you in similar  circumstances i guess did not  well i just also want to say i was  really lucky i was 22 years old when i  was married off some of those girls were  married  at 15 or 16 or 17. so if i had been that  young if i had been a child  just like them i don't think i would  have had the strength  to live i also knew of a number of girls  who actually did leave  a number of women who rebelled against  their families and said i'm not going to  be married off and i'm not going to do  what i'm told  and it ended badly for some of them uh  and i i still i'm in contact with  some of those girls and when i say badly  i mean  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai.tv